      "id": "rules",
      "title": "How to work on the hardware",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Box v2 is the only design being built (decided 2026-11-09). Its source of truth is `hardware/cad/box_v2.py`: every size is in its PARAMETERS block, and the build checks the fits and writes `hardware/print/box_v2*`. The camera housing, ultrasonic box and wheel sensor still take their dimensions from `hardware/cad/parameters.json`. v1 (the IP65 box with the printed tray, `hardware/cad/enclosure.py`, and the separate ultrasonic shells) is kept as documentation and a starting point only: do not print it or extend it. For the parts driven by `hardware/cad/parameters.json`, the dimensions are (labelled, with units and plain-language help). Edit them in the editor (`python3 hardware/knowledge/kb.py serve`): the 3D parts tab shows the model split into components (`hardware/cad/preview.py`), click a part and its dimensions open in the drawer on the right (focus a dimension to light up its part), then Save & update 3D view; Rebuild 3D files on the Dimensions page regenerates the STEP/STL/3MF. Or edit the JSON. Files in `hardware/print/` are generated. Never hand-edit an STL or STEP (STEP opens in FreeCAD as one dumb solid; change the parameters instead). To edit in FreeCAD itself, open the native files in `hardware/freecad/models/` (spreadsheet `Params` drives every feature; see `hardware/freecad/README.md`); they are generated from the same parameters by `freecadcmd hardware/freecad/build.py`.",
        "The editor opens on the Bike page (`hardware/knowledge/ui/bike.js`): a side view of the user's Gazelle Vento C7 drawn from the geometry in the bike section below, with every logger part where it sits. Click a tube to edit the geometry, click a part to edit where it sits and its CAD dimensions (the same parameters.json values as the 3D parts tab; Show in 3D jumps there), drag a part to move it on the drawing. The bike section's `mounts` x/y/w/h are drawing positions in mm from the rear axle, not measurements.",
        "The Wiring page (`hardware/knowledge/ui/wiring.js`) draws the Pi 5 header and ports, the breadboard and each sensor, with every row of the wiring table below as a wire. Click a wire, pin or hole to see or edit what connects there. When a pin, a sensor or the breadboard plan changes, change that table (and `config.toml` in a pull request if a GPIO number moves).",
        "Source of truth for *knowledge* (this file's content) is `hardware/knowledge/knowledge.json`. Edit it with `python3 hardware/knowledge/kb.py serve` or directly, then run `python3 hardware/knowledge/kb.py build`. Never hand-edit `hardware/CLAUDE.md`.",
        "Every printed part ships as editable parametric source plus STEP and STL (skill `cad-part`). Plates and print settings: skill `print-plate`. Keeping this file current: skill `project-notes`.",
        "Update this knowledge in the same commit as the change that makes it true. Record what the user said or measured; mark anything unverified as an estimate.",
        "Changes under `hardware/` and `.claude/` go straight to `main` (they never run on the bike). Changes to `bikelog/`, `web/`, `app/`, `deploy/`, `tests/` need a branch and a pull request.",
        "`ruff check .` lints `hardware/` too. The logger does not import it and the Pi does not need `cadquery`.",
        "A read-only copy of this editor is public at murathanyildirim.com/bike-logger/hardware/ (Netlify proxies it to `kb.py` with `KB_READONLY=1` on the API server, config in `hardware/knowledge/server/`). It does not update itself: after changing knowledge, parameters or print files, run `sh hardware/knowledge/deploy.sh` (ask the user first: it publishes). UI paths must stay relative (`api/...`, not `/api/...`) because the copy lives under a sub-path."
      ]
