    {
      "id": "decisions",
      "title": "Decisions",
      "intro": "Never delete a decision. If one is reversed, add the new one and say why.",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Keep the IP65 box; print only the tray inside it. Printed ASA cannot be promised watertight.",
        "No deck, no roof: with the breadboard flat there was 9-12 mm of headroom, too little for jumper wires. The breadboard stands on its edge so wires have a big open space in front.",
        "No back plate behind the breadboard: the box wall is the back. Glue it there (clean with isopropyl alcohol first).",
        "GPS stays inside the box for the prototype; test the fix with the lid closed, move it outside through a gland if it is poor.",
        "IMU on the floor beside the breadboard, hinged lid so it can be reached.",
        "Pi pocket raised 5 mm on a pedestal so the ports are not blocked by neighbouring pockets (only just enough, to keep the stack low). GPS moved into the +X/+Y corner for the best sky view and reach; the breadboard holder and IMU pocket shifted along Y to make room (layout is computed per tray variant by set_layout in enclosure.py).",
        "Final parts in eSUN ASA+ on the Bambu Lab P2S. PLA (Polymaker PolyTerra) is for test prints only: it softens in the sun.",
        "Rear camera lives in its own printed housing at the back of the bike, not in the main box. Window on the outside face so pressure seats it; blind mount hole so there is no leak path; lens opening only 15.6 mm because the PCB posts sit next to it (check for vignetting with the Wide lens).",
        "Tray cable holders rebuilt: the old arches had a 1.6 mm deep cradle cut across the cable direction and 2.6 mm tunnels, far too small for a bundle. Now 16 x 12 x 16 mm holders with a 12 mm wide, 9 mm deep groove along the cable and an 8 x 3.5 mm zip-tie tunnel under it (tie runs through the tunnel, up the outside of both walls and over the bundle).",
        "Tray cable holders rebuilt: the old arches had a 1.6 mm deep cradle cut across the cable direction and 2.6 mm tunnels, far too small for a bundle. Now 16 x 12 x 16 mm holders with a 12 mm wide, 9 mm deep groove along the cable and an 8 x 3.5 mm zip-tie tunnel under it (tie runs through the tunnel, up the outside of both walls and over the bundle).",
        "Cable trunk changed from four zip-tied holders to a covered channel with a snap-on lid (user request: openable, closable, open on the two short sides like a box). Reason: the lid closes the bundle in without zip ties and can be opened to add or change wires. Channel 12.8 x 15 mm inside, walls 2.4 mm, floor 2 mm; lid 2 mm plate with 4 snap tabs (1 mm thick, 6 mm tall) whose 0.4 mm bumps click into dimples in the walls. The channel starts 2 mm past the IMU pocket because the IMU lid and its latch need that room. The zip-tie tunnels were removed. Sizes are untested: check the snap force on a test print (PLA first).",
        "Ultrasonic control boards: one small box per board (`ultrasonic_box.py`), four printed next to each other, each with its own lid. Rejected after trying: pockets in the tray (no room), one shared hub with the boards upright, leaning or in a staircase (too complex). In the photos the 4-pin header is in the middle of one long edge of the board with the Dupont wires leaving straight out, and the probe plug sits on the back on the board's middle line with its cable running to one end. Chosen: the board (28.5 x 41 mm) lies with its long side along the box's long side, header edge towards a long wall; the four Dupont wires run along the strip beside the header, across a corridor at the cable end, to a notch in the exact middle of the SHORT -Y wall, open at the top (14 mm wide; the lid closes it, silicone after routing; the foam cord cannot cross it). The probe cable goes straight down through a 14 mm hole (doubled on request) in the EXACT CENTRE of the floor, with a short dent in the floor (the biggest face) so the plug sinks in and the board sits on short 3 mm posts; the cable runs along the dent from the plug end to the hole. The box stays compact, about 61 x 65 mm (an earlier version mirrored the box round the hole and reached about 79 x 100 mm, which the user found huge). Same sealing otherwise as the camera housing (foam cord, M3 inserts); the lid has pegs that hold the board.",
        "Tray foundation stiffened without growing: a 2 mm wide, 4 mm tall raised lip (LIP_W, LIP_H) runs round the outer edge of the 3 mm frame. Same 112.5 x 173.5 footprint, nothing inside moves, and the tray stays under the existing 32 mm tallest point. The lip is left out along the +X wall where the breadboard is glued, so the board still sits flat on the wall. Why: a flat 3 mm open frame twists easily; an edge lip raises bending stiffness a lot for about 5 g of plastic in total. Untested: check on a PLA print that the tray no longer flexes when lifted by one corner; raise LIP_H or add cross ribs if it does.",
        "Cable channel and its lid removed for now (user request). Kept in the code behind CH_ON (0 = off, 1 = on) so it can come back: with it off, the tray, plates and print files have no channel and no cable lid. Cables are free in the box until the channel is decided again.",
        "Ultrasonic box shrunk (user: far too oversized for a 29 x 41 mm board): foam-cord gasket groove dropped (the lid seals with a silicone bead; US_GW = 0 turns the groove off, set it back to 3.2 and US_WALL to 1.6 to bring it back), single 2.4 mm wall, Dupont room 17 to 14.6 mm, end corridor 8 to 5 mm, board width 28.5 to 29 mm. Box went from 61 x 65 to 51 x 54 mm (60 x 63 with the ears). Centred floor hole and the side notch are unchanged. Untested: bath test with silicone only.",
        "Ultrasonic box redone as a tight shell (user: no empty space, no 4 screws). No Dupont plugs: the wires are soldered to the header and leave straight out through a notch (US_WIRE_D, 14 mm after the user asked to widen it; open at the top) in the +X wall next to the header, so there is no plug room and no wire corridor. No screws, ears, inserts or foam cord: the lid has a 3 mm lip that presses into the opening (0.15 mm per side) and is sealed with silicone. Cavity is the 41 x 29 mm board plus 0.4 mm per side, 2.4 mm walls. Box 35 x 47 x 30 mm (was 61 x 65 x 28). Centred floor hole kept. Untested: press-fit tightness and a bath test; if the lid is loose, add a silicone bead or two small snap bumps.",
        "Pi pocket pushed all the way into the -X/-Y corner (user request: it does not need free space on both sides). This frees the +Y end, where one ultrasonic shell now sits in a pocket, upside down so the probe cable hole faces the box lid (the cable leaves through a gland in the lid). IMU lid still opens 90 degrees clear of the Pi; on the notched tray the Pi sits just above the post cut-out and the rim strip was lengthened to keep the tray one piece. Untested: the shell fit in its pocket (0.4 mm per side, friction plus a dab of silicone), and 27 mm for the USB plugs at the Pi's end. The notched tray has no ultrasonic pocket (US_TRAY applies to the standard tray only). Parameters: PI_CORNER, US_TRAY, US_T_WALL, US_T_H.",
        "Wheel sensor: one printed bracket, no screws, on the REAR wheel (the user's bike is a Gazelle Vento: closed chain case on the drive side, roller brake on the left of the hub, panniers and an AXA frame lock over the upper wheel). It clamps to the left (non-drive side) seatstay a few cm above the dropout and the roller brake (the spot the user chose), on the stay's inner side, and reads a magnet on a left-side spoke near the hub; the pannier only touches the stay's outer side. The clamp takes a rounded-rectangle tube section (width, depth, corner radius: covers round, oval and flattened stays); it is held by two 2.5 mm zip-ties in grooves on its outside, with a strip of inner tube as pad. An arm carries a 1.6 mm window pocket for the KY-003 board, chip against the window, facing the spokes, 8 mm chip-to-magnet (the A3144 is weak: about 10 mm is the limit). The pocket is open at one end: fit it facing up so the wires run up the seatstay; fill it with silicone after soldering. Sensor on 3V3, never 5 V. (First version clamped to a round front fork leg.)",
        "Wheel sensor clamp takes a D-shaped stay (user checked the tube, 2026-10-18): the stay is flat on the side facing the wheel and rounded on the outer side, not a round tube. The tube section now has two corner radii: WS_TUBE_R on the wheel side (where the clamp's C seats; flat, ESTIMATE 3 mm) and WS_TUBE_R_OUT on the outer side (round, half the depth minus 0.2). The clamp only touches the wheel side, so the flat face gives it a flat seat that cannot rotate on the stay; the zip-ties go round the rounded outer side. Untested: test-fit once W, H and the two radii are measured.",
        "Wheel sensor stay is now a true D, replacing the two-radius rounded rectangle of the previous decision (user: the clamp looked like it was holding a cube). Section: flat face towards the wheel with corner radius WS_TUBE_R, and a half-ellipse from the end of those corners round to the back; W sideways (flat face to the back), H front to back. WS_TUBE_R_OUT was dropped (the ellipse follows from W, H and R). The clamp's bore follows that shape and its back corners are rounded with the bore, so the C hugs the stay. Because the D is widest a little inside the clamp's mouth, the stay snaps in (0.6 mm per side with the guessed sizes; the build stops above 1.5 mm); the zip-ties still hold it.",
        "Wheel sensor fit test: the clamp alone is its own print file (`wheel_sensor_fit.step/.stl/.3mf`, about 3 g), built by `build_clamp()` from the same parameters as the bracket, so a change in the editor updates both. In the 3D view the bracket is split into two components, Clamp (= the fit test) and Arm and sensor holder. Print the fit test, try it on the stay, adjust the stay numbers, then print the bracket.",
        "Wheel sensor cable slot (user: the wires come out of the board on the side that was completely closed): a rectangular hole through the MIDDLE of the sensor pocket's floor (the closed end opposite the open top), the full pocket depth and WS_CABLE_W wide (10 mm: three soldered wires or a 3-pin Dupont plug). The board still rests on the floor either side of it (at least 2 mm, checked by the build). The top stays open to put the board in; silicone fills both after soldering.",
        "Wheel sensor stay depth MEASURED: the flat face of the D-shaped stay is 23 mm (WS_TUBE_H 16 -> 23). The first fit-test print (16 mm guess) was too small to go on. Clamp and fit test are now 31.4 mm across; the mouth snaps 0.8 mm per side. Width sideways (WS_TUBE_W, 16) and the flat-face corner radius (WS_TUBE_R, 3) are still guesses.",
        "Rear camera redesigned for the Camera Module 3 Wide from the official Raspberry Pi mechanical drawing (2026-10-18), replacing the estimated sizes, and mounted on the back end of the rear rack (user: connected to the iron at the back that holds the main box, at its end). The 1/4\" TNSLAND hole and the ribbon slot in the floor are gone. Mount: 90 deg V on top of the housing plus a printed cap and two M3 screws (rigid, so the video does not shake; the V centres any tube from 8 to 16 mm and lets the housing turn to aim). The board sits upright with its connector at the top, so the ribbon goes out of the lid towards the bike and the picture needs no rotation. The lens barrel now reaches into the front wall (glass 0.5 mm behind the window) because the Wide lens sees 120 deg diagonally; the old 15.6 mm opening 1 mm in front of the lens would have shown its edges. Assumed: the rack's back end is a round tube running sideways (10 mm ESTIMATE).",
        "Camera ribbon goes straight down through the housing floor (2026-10-25, user: the ribbon is not a normal cable and cannot be curved inside, so the hole must be exactly where it leaves the connector). The connector is on the back of the board, flush with the edge, and the ribbon leaves it in the board's plane; with the board upright it would go straight up into the rack tube and clamp, so the board is now mounted ribbon-down (connector at the bottom) and the 20 x 3 mm slot moved from the lid to the floor, right under the connector at y = back of PCB + 1.29 mm (half the 2.58 mm connector height). Room under the board 5 -> 3 mm (no bend needed). The script checks that a 16 mm ribbon running straight down from the connector touches neither housing, lid nor cap. Ribbon-down is the normal mounting for a Pi camera, so no picture rotation is expected; if the first picture is upside down, set rpiCameraHFlip and rpiCameraVFlip in mediamtx.yml.",
        "Camera lid snaps on, no screws (2026-10-25, user: make the lid lockable; chose snap latches over keeping the screws). Four cantilever latches, two per side at z = +-10 mm: arm 14 x 1.8 x 9 mm, hook 1.1 mm deep behind a 1.0 mm ridge with a 3 mm ramp; bending strain 1.9 % (the script stops above 2 % for ASA). The four screw ears and lid inserts are gone, so the housing is 41.8 mm wide (49 with the arms) instead of 55. A lip inside the cavity locates the lid. Print the lid outer face down (arms up); the hook and ridge faces are 1 mm overhangs, fine without supports.",
        "Camera lid snap latches replaced by a slide lock (2026-10-25, user: printed plastic is fragile and snap arms break over time; asked for simple sliding but robust). Nothing flexes: four solid tongues (4 x 2.5 x 1.6 mm) on two short side walls of the lid go into L-shaped grooves in the housing's side walls (side walls 1.6 mm thicker, C_SIDE, so the grooves stay 1.6 mm clear of the gasket groove). Press on, push down 7 mm; a 0.5 mm ramp over the last 2.7 mm pulls the lid onto the cord (slope 0.19, self-locking), and gravity keeps it locked because the housing hangs. The lid is 7 mm longer at the bottom so it covers the cord during the whole slide; its side walls start 0.5 mm in front of the rack tube so it opens on the bike. The lid pegs had to go (they would scrape across the board), so the board's pins are 1 mm longer for heat staking. The script checks the lid goes on and slides down without touching the housing or the tube, that the ledges hold it when locked, and that the ramp stays self-locking.",
        "Camera lid: the slide lock is replaced by two lock bars (2026-10-25, user: the four lid pegs must stay, they hold the chip in place; a lid that slides would drag them across the board). The lid goes straight on again with its pegs and lip; two separate rigid C-shaped bars slide down over a solid rail on each side of the housing and a flange on the lid, clamping them together. Still nothing flexes: the rail's front face tapers 0.6 mm over 34.6 mm (slope 0.017) so the bar wedges the lid onto the cord and cannot work loose, and gravity holds the bars down because the housing hangs. The heat-staked pins and the 1.6 mm thicker side walls of the slide lock are gone. The bars are one part (camera_lock) printed twice, right and mirrored left, lying on their back.",
        "Print plates are written by hardware/cad/plate3mf.py (2026-10-25): trimesh's 3MF writer leaves out build items whose node name starts with 'camera' (it takes them for scene cameras), so camera_plate.3mf had no parts on the plate and Bambu Studio said it contains no geometry. Build nodes now get a 'part_' prefix (object names unchanged), the layout is centred on the 256 x 256 plate, and the writer checks every part is on the build list.",
        "Box v2 (2026-11-07, user: forget the existing box and tray, design a whole new box and tray around the chips, with lots of cables and sensor boards inside, robust on the back of a bicycle). This reverses 'keep the IP65 box; print only the tray': the box itself is now printed (`hardware/cad/box_v2.py`), so it can be shaped round the electronics and the rack. The v1 tray and IP65 box files stay as they are. Layout: inside 216 x 156 x 78 mm (body 240 x 180 x 81, lid 245 x 185; sized so every part fits the 256 mm P2S plate). Two levels: the power bank lies on the floor in a cradle, now INSIDE the box (charged through a sealed USB-C port in the front wall), with the IMU on the floor beside it, fixed to the box itself. A removable 3 mm deck on six columns (M3 inserts) carries row A: Pi 5 lying flat, 26 mm for its USB plugs, GPS turned 90 degrees; row B: breadboard lying flat (25 mm for jumper wires above it), a card rack holding the four ultrasonic boards upright on their long edge, 14 mm apart, header up, probe plug towards -X (the four separate ultrasonic shells are no longer needed). An 18 mm cable lane between the rows has five zip-tie anchors and a slot down to the bank; the deck stops 6 mm short of the walls so cables drop past it; the first 22 mm inside each end wall stay clear for gland nuts and cable bends. Sealing: 3 mm silicone foam cord in a 3.6 x 3.0 mm groove on the wall top, a 2.8 mm tongue on the lid squeezes it 33 %; 14 M3 screws into inserts in a 12 mm top band OUTSIDE the cord (no leak path); a skirt on the lid covers the seam; a 45 degree chamfer under the band so it prints without supports; desiccant cage under the lid. Openings only on the end walls, low: rear = four M16 glands (probes) and a 20 x 3 mm ribbon slot with a screwed clamp plate; front = charge port, power button, wheel sensor gland (all 16.2 mm). Mount: the box screws to a shoe through four blind floor bosses (no holes through the floor); the shoe slides onto two 45 degree dovetail rails on a rack plate, from the front, and one horizontal M3 screw through a tab at the back locks it. The rack plate (240 x 150) is strapped to the rack with 12 mm hose clamps or UV ties through rows of slots, so it suits any rail spacing. The build checks one valid solid per part, no overlap between parts and the electronics, gland nuts and keep-out zones, cord squeeze 25-45 %, insert wall to the groove, and that each plate fits the P2S. Untested: a bath test of the printed seal (30 min under water with a tissue inside) before any ride.",
        "Box v2 ultrasonic boards moved from the deck card rack to the rear wall (2026-11-07, user: the boards cannot stand 14 mm apart; the probe input sits in the middle of the board and points out at 90 degrees, so the plug and its cable had no room; also the viewer opened half exploded, so the box looked as if it could not close). Each board now stands upright (41 mm vertical) 18 mm in front of the inside of the rear wall with its plug facing the wall, and its M16 gland sits exactly on the plug's axis: plug (8 mm) -> 4 mm of straight cable -> gland nut (6 mm) -> wall, no bend inside the box. The gland positions are computed from the board positions and US_PLUG_OFF, so measuring the plug position moves the glands with it. Bottom edges sit in 1.9 mm slots in a ledge on the rear wall (45 degree underside, prints without supports); a separate clamp bar over the top edges is held by two M3 screws into inserts in end posts. The 4-pin headers (middle of a long edge) face each other in pairs across a 10 mm gap for the soldered wires; 8 mm between the pairs. Knock-on changes: inside width 156 -> 164 mm (body 188 wide, lid 193) for the clamp-post inserts; the deck now ends at x = 78 (30 mm short of the rear wall), the rear deck columns moved to x = 70; the GPS moved to row B, and row A behind the Pi's USB plugs is a spare bay with tie slots; the camera ribbon slot moved from the rear wall to low in the +Y side wall, under the deck. The front keep-clear zone is 16 mm. The viewer now opens assembled and has a cut-through slider. Checks added: plug on the gland axis, gland hex clear of the band chamfer and on the flat of the wall, clamp-post insert wall, deck clear of the boards.",
        "Box v2 rain hoods (2026-11-07, user: the holes need a cover so rain does not get in). Each end wall gets a removable printed hood over its openings: a roof that falls 4 mm towards its front edge so water runs off outwards, two side cheeks, a front face, and an open bottom so the cables leave downwards and you can reach the charge port and button from below. Hoods stand 38 mm out (the glands and ports outside the wall are taken as 22 mm, ESTIMATE, plus room for the cable to turn down); the roof sits just under the band chamfer, so water from the lid skirt and the band drips onto it and off its front edge. Fixed with M3 x 16 through counterbored posts inside the hood into blind inserts in bosses on the inside of the wall (no hole through the wall): three at the rear between the probe glands, two at the front. The ribbon clamp grew its own roof and cheeks. The front openings moved down 6 mm (38 mm above the floor) so their outside parts stay under the hood roof. The glands still do the sealing; the hoods keep rain and wheel spray off them. Prints: each hood on its front face, plate 6. Checks added: hood clear of the body, lid, rack plate and of every gland or port body outside the wall, and deep enough for a cable bend.",
        "Box v2 sizes updated (2026-11-07). Power bank MEASURED by the user: 152 x 72 mm (was the 126 x 63 guess); thickness still the 27 mm estimate. AJ-SR04M from a retailer spec (Fabian, Malta, Haitronic listing): 42 x 29 x 18 mm overall, so the board is 42 long (was 41) and front parts + PCB + probe socket take 18 mm. No source gives the split or the hole positions; assumed 6 mm of parts in front, 1.2 mm PCB, 11 mm socket and plug behind. With the deeper boards the deck now ends at x = 74 (was 78) and the rear columns moved to x = 66. Outer size unchanged; all checks pass.",
        "Box v2 lid snap clips (2026-11-09, user: the lid should clip on without screws too, but keep the screws). Ten cantilever arms are cut out of the lid skirt by a 1 mm slot either side: four on each long side (x = -66, -22, 22, 66) and one in the middle of each short side, all between the 14 screws. Each arm is 12 mm wide, 2 mm thick (the skirt) and runs 12 mm down from the lid underside, past the skirt, to a hook that reaches 0.7 mm into the band, with a 45 degree lead-in. The hook catches under a ledge cut into the band's outer face (1 mm deep, flat ceiling 0.1 mm above the hook with the lid down, open downwards into the band chamfer, so the body still prints without supports). Press the lid down and it clicks shut and holds the cord squeezed; the 14 M3 screws still go in for full clamping force. To open without screws, pull each arm tip outwards (it hangs 4 mm below the skirt). Bending strain 1.7 % (the build stops above 2 % for ASA); the build checks the clips sit on the flat of each wall, clear of the screw inserts, and that the lid closes with no overlap. Snap travel checked by lifting the lid in steps: only the hooks meet the band. Parameters CLIP_* in box_v2.py.",
        "Box v2 rain cover checked and tightened (2026-11-09, user: make sure the holes of the body are covered at the top for rain). The build now casts rays up from just outside every opening (the four probe glands, the three front holes, the ribbon slot, pad and clamp), straight up and slanted 45 degrees towards the wall: every ray hits the band, a hood or the ribbon visor (the build stops if one gets through). They already did; what was left was water running along surfaces, so three changes: (1) a drip notch all round under the band (1.5 mm wide, 1 mm drip face, 1.5 mm in from the edge), so water creeping along the 45 degree chamfer drops onto the hood roofs instead of reaching the wall; (2) the hood roofs and cheeks now stand 1 mm off the wall for a 1 mm foam strip that seals the seam, while the screw posts sit on the wall; (3) the ribbon visor roof now reaches back over the pad and the clamp to the wall (same 1 mm foam seam) and falls 1.5 mm outwards, so no water sits on the pad top above the foam seal. The ribbon clamp now prints on its side. The glands and ports still do the sealing.",
        "Box v2 is the only design from now on (2026-11-09, user: we will only use v2 but keep the v1 documentation as a starting point). Not built any more: the IP65 box with the printed tray (`enclosure.py`, plates 1 and 2, IMU lid), the separate ultrasonic shells (`ultrasonic_box.py`, now on box v2's rear wall), the power bank in its own housing (now inside box v2) and the `rear_box.py` draft. Their scripts, files and notes stay as they are, marked v1 / reference, so an idea can be picked up again from there. The camera housing and the wheel sensor are not part of the box and stay current. The editor's 3D view now opens on box v2, and the Bike page draws it on the rack."
